 * A regular expression in a setting can now have at most 100 capture groups.
   This applies to the pattern of the var-expand
   [[link,settings_variables_filters,`regexp` filter]], to the pattern of
   the `~` and `!~` comparators of the `if` and `switch` filters, and to the
   regular expression of [[setting,sieve_spamtest_status_header]],
   [[setting,sieve_virustest_status_header]] and, when they are in use,
   [[setting,sieve_spamtest_score_max_header]] and
   [[setting,sieve_virustest_score_max_header]]. A var-expand pattern
   with more groups fails with `too many capture groups (maximum 100)` when
   the setting is expanded, which is at lookup time rather than when the
   configuration is read, so `doveconf` does not report it. Previously
   `regexp` replaced the first match of such a pattern and `~` never matched
   it, so `!~` always did. The Sieve settings are checked when Sieve loads
   the spamtest or virustest extension, which needs
   [[setting,sieve_extensions]], [[setting,sieve_global_extensions]] or
   [[setting,sieve_implicit_extensions]] to enable it. A pattern with more
   groups logs an `Invalid status header specification` error (`Invalid max
   score header specification` for the `score_max_header` settings) and Sieve
   fails to initialize. Then no script runs: delivery through the LDA and LMTP
   saves the mail in the default mailbox, and ManageSieve disconnects the
   client with an internal error while the interpreter message goes to the
   log. If the setting is only inside a protocol filter, only delivery through
   that protocol breaks. The ManageSieve capabilities are generated by
   initializing Sieve while the configuration is read, so otherwise `doveconf`
   and a configuration reload fail with `Failed to initialize Sieve`, and on a
   restart the config service does not start, so no service can read the
   configuration. Setting [[setting,managesieve_sieve_capability]],
   [[setting,managesieve_notify_capability]] and
   `managesieve_extlists_capability` explicitly avoids this. Change the groups
   that do not need to be captured into non-capturing groups: `(?:...)`.
 * The group limit no longer rejects a pattern with exactly 100 capture groups
   in the `~` and `!~` comparators. Previously `~` was false for it and `!~`
   true whenever the match stayed within the matching depth limit. Matching 100
   consecutive capture groups can still fail the expansion with `matching depth
   limit exceeded`, also at lookup time, and `!~` is then not true.
